Make SELinux permanently permissive

enforcing_enabled() is the single predicate every SELinux enforcement
decision passes through. In Linux 6.6 there are exactly five readers:

  security/selinux/avc.c:1014        avc_denied()
  security/selinux/hooks.c:5950      unrecognized-netlink fallback
  security/selinux/ss/services.c:744  security_validate_transition()
  security/selinux/ss/services.c:1665 compute_sid_handle_invalid_context()
  security/selinux/ss/services.c:1972 convert_context_handle_invalid_context()

plus security/selinux/selinuxfs.c, which only reports the value. Returning
false here therefore makes the whole LSM log-and-allow without touching a
single hook, a single struct, or a single exported symbol - so it is kABI
neutral by construction.

This deliberately does NOT touch enforcing_set(), so selinux_state.enforcing
is still written and /sys/fs/selinux/enforce stays writable: setenforce(8)
keeps returning success, it simply has no effect.

This is not selinux=0. selinuxfs is still registered and the policy is still
loaded, which is what Android init requires in order to boot at all.

--- a/security/selinux/include/security.h
+++ b/security/selinux/include/security.h
@@ -124,7 +124,25 @@
 #ifdef CONFIG_SECURITY_SELINUX_DEVELOP
 static inline bool enforcing_enabled(void)
 {
-	return READ_ONCE(selinux_state.enforcing);
+	/*
+	 * Permanently permissive build.
+	 *
+	 * Every enforcement decision in SELinux funnels through this one
+	 * predicate - avc_denied(), the netlink-message fallback in hooks.c,
+	 * security_validate_transition(), compute_sid_handle_invalid_context()
+	 * and convert_context_handle_invalid_context() are the only readers -
+	 * so returning false here makes the whole LSM log-and-allow without
+	 * touching a single hook.
+	 *
+	 * selinux_state.enforcing is deliberately still written by
+	 * enforcing_set(), so /sys/fs/selinux/enforce stays writable and
+	 * userspace setenforce(8) keeps returning success. It just has no
+	 * effect: getenforce reports Permissive again on the next read.
+	 *
+	 * selinuxfs is still mounted and the policy is still loaded, which is
+	 * what Android init requires to boot. This is NOT selinux=0.
+	 */
+	return false;
 }

 static inline void enforcing_set(bool value)
